# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# Two groups, and the split is deliberate.
#
# THE ENFORCED ONES are the headers that cannot break a working app: they forbid things this app
# never does. Shipping them enforcing from the first deploy costs nothing.
#
# THE CONTENT SECURITY POLICY IS REPORT-ONLY, and should stay that way until the reports are quiet.
# A CSP is the one header that breaks an application silently — a blocked script is a feature that
# quietly stops existing, with the reason in a console nobody has open. This app loads a third-party
# stylesheet, runs WebAssembly, opens sockets to several hosts, renders a QR code by *fetching* a
# data: URI, and embeds games in iframes; every one of those is a directive that has to be right.
# Report-Only exercises the whole policy against real traffic and blocks nothing.
#
# REPORTS GO TO SENTRY, which the app already uses — `report-uri` below. Without it the policy was
# decorative: Firefox says so outright ("has a Report-Only policy without a report-uri or report-to
# directive; CSP will not block and cannot report violations"), and the only trace of a violation
# was a console message on whichever machine happened to hit it.
#
# `report-to` is deliberately absent. It is the modern replacement, but it delivers a different
# payload shape through the Reporting API, and where a browser supports it it IGNORES `report-uri`
# entirely — so adding it would swap working reports in Chrome for a format Sentry may drop on the
# floor. When Sentry's endpoint documents Reporting API support, add `Reporting-Endpoints` and
# `report-to csp` together and drop this paragraph.
#
# To enforce it: watch the reports across real routes — a call, a game in its iframe, the QR code —
# then rename the header to `Content-Security-Policy`. Both may be sent at once during a changeover:
# the browser honours the enforcing one and reports on the other. Expect noise from browser
# extensions injecting scripts into the page; that is not the app violating its own policy.

/*
  # An asset whose Content-Type is wrong is not to be guessed at; that guess is how an uploaded
  # image becomes a script.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  # Referrers are useful to our own origin and nobody else's business. `strict-origin-...` also stops
  # a path leaking over the wire when leaving https.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # Nothing here is meant to be framed. `frame-ancestors` in the CSP below says the same thing to
  # anything modern; this covers what is left.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

  # The app asks for the microphone and the camera itself, from its own origin — everything else is
  # denied outright so an embedded game cannot ask on its behalf.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

  # REPORT-ONLY — see the note at the top before enforcing.
  #
  #   script-src      'wasm-unsafe-eval' because the app runs WebAssembly, which is in the boot
  #                   gate's required list. Deliberately NOT 'unsafe-eval': nothing should need it,
  #                   and the reports are how we find out whether something does.
  #                   The sha256 is the theme bootstrap in index.html, which has to be inline to run
  #                   before the first paint. It is SUBSTITUTED AT BUILD TIME by scripts/cspHeaders.ts
  #                   from the emitted file — never edit it here, and never delete the token.
  #                   cloudflareinsights is Pages' own Web Analytics beacon, injected into the
  #                   response by the CDN rather than by this repository. Turning Web Analytics off
  #                   in the dashboard is the other way to satisfy this line.
  #   style-src       'unsafe-inline' is not optional — Vue writes inline styles for every scoped
  #                   rule and transition — and emoji-css is the third-party sheet index.html pulls.
  #   connect-src     data: is not a mistake: qr-code-styling loads the logo in the middle of the QR
  #                   by fetching it, and the bundler inlines that SVG as a data: URI. Listed under
  #                   img-src alone, the QR code silently loses its centre.
  #                   wss: for the realtime socket and for LiveKit's SFU.
  #   frame-src       blob: for the playframe host; games themselves are served from this origin.
  #   worker-src      blob: because vite's worker format produces blob-backed workers.
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' {{INLINE_SCRIPT_HASHES}} https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://emoji-css.afeld.me; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.argon.gl https://*.argon.zone; media-src 'self' blob: https://*.argon.gl; font-src 'self' data: https://emoji-css.afeld.me; connect-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.argon.gl wss://*.argon.gl https://*.argon.zone wss://*.argon.zone https://cloudflareinsights.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob:; child-src 'self' blob:; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; report-uri https://sentry.argon.gl/api/22/security/?sentry_key=9d074ba73b580b47cdcc16adf72f523d

# The service worker is served from the root so it can claim the whole origin, and it must never be
# held by a cache — a stale one keeps serving an old cache strategy long after a deploy.
/media-sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
